course: dictionary
lesson: 11549
title: Amicable: friendly, without a fight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Amicable means friendly and calm, with no arguing or fighting. It is most useful when people could have argued but did not. «The two friends had a small disagreement, but they settled it amicably.» The word amicably tells you they sorted it out peacefully and kindly.
explain: Look for clues around the word. «The neighbours talked about the fence and agreed on a fair plan without any shouting.» The words without any shouting show that amicable means friendly and peaceful. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try friendly in the sentence instead and check it still makes sense.
explain: The word also describes endings. «The couple parted amicably and still said hello in the park.» Parted means went their separate ways, and amicably tells you they did it calmly and without bitterness. Amicable is an adjective, and amicably is the adverb that tells you how something was done.
fact: Amicable means friendly and without arguments, especially when people could have argued.
fact: «Amicably» is the adverb: «They settled the problem amicably» means they settled it in a calm, friendly way.
fact: An amicable agreement is one that both sides agree to calmly, without a fight.
fact: Amicable can describe people, a relationship, a split, or a settlement.
fact: Similar words are friendly and peaceful, but amicable is the word to use when a disagreement ended calmly.
exercise: «The two friends had a small disagreement, but they settled it amicably.» What does amicably mean here? | Loudly, with lots of shouting | Quickly, without thinking first | *Calmly and without fighting | Sadly, with tears
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to amicable? | Angry | Lost | *Friendly | Tired
exercise: «They made an amicable agreement about sharing the toys.» Find and copy the word that describes the agreement. | agreement | Sharing | Toys | *amicable
test: «After the football match, both teams shook hands and parted amicably.» How did the teams part? | Without speaking at all | Angrily | In a hurry, running off | *Peacefully and in a friendly way
test: Which sentence uses amicable correctly? | Amicable is a kind of cake. | The players argued and shouted all day, so the game was amicable. | The amicable dog ran away and hid under the bed. | *The neighbours sorted out the fence in an amicable way.
exam: What does amicable mean? | *Friendly and without arguments, especially when people could have argued | Short and rude | Sad and lonely | Very large and loud
